.docname {Releasing}
.include {docs}

Releases are built from tags. CI builds Linux and Windows; the macOS build needs an Apple machine and is made by hand.

## 1. Tag the release

```shell
git tag v0.2.0 && git push origin v0.2.0
```

On a tag, CI runs the usual lint and test stages, then:

| Job               | Output                                         | Notes                                    |
|-------------------|------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `release-linux`   | `gol-bevy-<tag>-x86_64-linux.tar.gz`           | Built on Debian trixie, so it needs glibc 2.39 or newer |
| `release-windows` | `gol-bevy-<tag>-x86_64-windows.zip`            | Cross-compiled with MinGW, so no Windows runner is needed |
| `release`         | A GitLab release named `gol-bevy <tag>`        | Links to both archives                   |

Release binaries are built with `--locked` and stripped of symbols, which are about a third of the binary. Local release builds keep them for profiling. The archives are kept as job artifacts with no expiry, since the release page links to them.

## 2. Build for macOS

On a Mac:

```shell
just release-macos v0.2.0
```

This adds both Apple targets, builds for Apple silicon and Intel, joins them into a universal binary with `lipo`, and writes `dist/gol-bevy-v0.2.0-universal-macos.tar.gz`. Without a version, it uses `git describe --tags --always`.

## 3. Attach the macOS archive

Edit the release on GitLab and drag the macOS archive into its description.

## Archive layout

`scripts/package-release.sh` packages every platform the same way:

```shell
scripts/package-release.sh <archive name> <binary> [tar.gz|zip]
```

It copies the binary, `README.md`, and `LICENSE` into a folder named after the archive, packs it into `dist/`, and prints the archive path.

.filetree
    - dist
      - gol-bevy-v0.2.0-x86_64-linux.tar.gz
        - gol-bevy-v0.2.0-x86_64-linux
          - gol-bevy
          - README.md
          - LICENSE
